WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Marble Madness!

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.640
Roll to the goal in a race against time!

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:27.900
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.840
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.100
I'm Sean. I'm Joe.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.560
And I'm Sam.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.760
Sean, can we get an update on the Marble Madness Manic episodes that you've been having as of late?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.380
You mean you want me to talk about my psychological health right now?

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.960
If, you know, I was wondering if...

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.760
You want me to disclose this information on a podcast?

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.160
I wanted to know if it's gotten any better now that you've played the game,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.440
if maybe that over...

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.140
you know, like you overcame the disease.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.840
No, no, no. Exposure therapy does not work.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.640
I'm even worse now.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.400
But thanks for telling all of our listeners.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.540
Well, for those of you that are like, what is Mike even talking about?

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.220
Sean has been officially diagnosed with Marble Madness by a doctor pretty recently.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.540
And it's a lot like Pac-Man fever, if you will.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.340
Yeah. There is also a fever element to it.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.140
Joe, Sam, you don't have to be so quiet.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.880
It's okay. Sean's going to recover.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.400
I'm just...

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.140
I just feel so bad.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:38.120
I... yeah. Yeah, we got very little to contribute between the two of us.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.680
You'll be okay. It's just marbles.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.840
Marbles. Yeah, you don't want to lose your marbles over a game like Marble Madness.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:52.660
This is a platform game in which the player is a marble and you've got to guide this marble...

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.240
Actually, wait a minute. That's a good question.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.160
Let's just hold the phone here on the overview.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.640
Are you the marble or are you controlling the marble as God?

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:08.280
I think you're the marble in the same sense you're any other character you play like in a third person game.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.340
Yeah, I think you're the marble.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.480
You bring up a good... you bring up a good point about like the theory of the self and of the mind.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:16.520
Like are we...

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:22.460
are we just a complex interconnection of like ourselves or are we a person?

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:28.020
Is the marble us or are we just pressing buttons to influence a marble?

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.460
I thought of myself as localized wind.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.080
Just blowing the marble here and there.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:40.520
I mean, that's kind of what I'm getting at Sam is that, you know, with Super Mario Brothers, you know, you're Mario.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.600
It's not up for discussion.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.480
You're not Mario's conscious.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:44.760
You know, like you're just...

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.660
you are Mario.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.980
Mark Rider, you know, you are Mark Rider.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:49.260
Yeah, you are Mark Rider.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.440
The game told you that.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:51.640
Yeah. Joe specifically.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.280
I haven't forgotten. And in this game though, it's like it's a marble.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.540
So I don't think you should just immediately assume that you are the marble.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.800
And my theory is you're the marble, but...

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.420
Maybe you just think you're the marble and that's why it's marble madness.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.580
And the whole thing is just a fever dream that Sean is having.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.780
You play as Sean.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:23.220
See, I feel like if the stage were like tilting or something as you moved the way you control it, then I would be a little more, I'd be questioning, okay, am I the marble or am I the stage?

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.700
Or am I just like I control it, trying to manipulate the marble?

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.160
But since it's not, I mean, I feel like I'm the marble.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.460
Oh, you're talking about like Super Monkey Ball.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.220
Yeah. You're the stage.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.400
Or, you know, those little things that like that you can get for a dollar at a knockoff store or something.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.280
Okay, well, Sean, don't be ridiculous.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.040
If you can buy it in real life, you're obviously not the marble.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.140
This is part of his marble madness symptoms.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.560
He can't tell in real life if he's the marbles or not.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.380
I am the marble.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:09.240
To get back to on track here, Marble Madness, platform game in which you guide a marble through courses populated with obstacles and everything.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.820
Like calling them enemies is kind of silly, but they're more enemy-like obstacles.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.220
Shapes. Yeah.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.840
And you got to do this in a set time limit per stage.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.080
I think like the faster you complete the stage, the more points you get.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.900
But also like that time, correct me if I'm wrong here, it kind of gets like added to your next stage.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:29.840
It carries over, yeah. Yeah.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:34.540
So it's also important to do this fast because you need to build upon your time.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.820
Did you guys feel like that was a fair evaluation of marble madness?

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:47.400
That is indeed what you do in marble madness, whether you are of the camp that you are the marble or if you're just influencing a marble.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:53.000
I think it's also important to say that it's like a 3D world too.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.440
It's not too much.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.400
It's like an isometric, yeah.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.000
Yeah. It's like a top-down isometric, not top-down, isometric 3D world.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.400
Yeah, that is important to say because that's going to control a lot of the movement in this game.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:14.480
The isometric is kind of similar to the kart racing game that Rare also made.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.580
Oh, that too. What's that?

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.060
I was also going to say Q-Bert.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.320
Oh, Q-Bert.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:19.560
Yes, very true.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.020
I'm just linking Rare here together with RC Pro-Am, which was another one of their games.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:26.900
That is correct.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.380
Now, Rare didn't originally make this game though, but I think it's just worth mentioning.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.680
You could see the parallels here.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.140
So, we're going to talk about the marble first.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.360
It doesn't matter if you are the marble or not.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:39.400
Don't worry about that.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.620
Unless you're going to have an existential crisis about it, in which case, you know, I'm sure there's a help hotline for that.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.060
The podcast can't really help you.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.760
You guys keep making light of the situation and I really don't appreciate it.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.820
Anyway, let's talk about movement of the marble because that's pretty important, right?

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:57.240
If the whole game is about moving the marble, how does it feel to control this marble?

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.740
Joe, any immediate thoughts come to mind about controlling the marble?

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.440
I'll say it takes a second to kind of get used to it.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:14.120
I mean, it's just the fact that you're playing, you know, you're controlling a marble using a D-pad and you don't have a joystick.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.720
Right off the bat, it gives you like some little issues.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:28.220
But I will say that once you sort of get the hang of it, you know, once you get the feel and understand like the nuances of how it controls, it feels pretty good.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.400
Yeah, I'd say it's also a like a physics simulation too.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.060
It's not just like it feels very realistic.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.500
It feels like when you move, you're not just moving your character, you're adding force in a direction.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:50.200
Yeah, there's definitely momentum and like a curve to the speed at which you move.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.060
The game does a lot of graphics work too to simulate that feeling for you, right Sam?

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:11.920
You know, you were saying that it's physics based, but you can also kind of see the motion of your marble through that depth that they add on the top of the marble, you know, where the light source would hit it.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:18.120
But you can kind of see that move around as well, which helps let you know how out of control you are.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.040
Yeah, your marble also has what is it like a ring of stars inside of it or something.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:31.000
It's got some kind of design inside the marble itself, I think, that you can see move around that lets you know that it's a sphere.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:42.320
Yeah, and that kind of gives you also some hints as to what direction you're rolling, though you can see the marble rolling anyway.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.180
But it definitely makes it feel a bit more like a solid object.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:08:04.020
Perfect. And like the way that the marble controls too, like you get like the two options of like sort of what perspective you want to treat it, where you can like sort of play it as if it were Q-Bert with that weird like 45 degree angle thing.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:11.280
Or just sort of like a one-to-one, what you see on the screen is the direction you're going to go sort of thing.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.240
Right, it gives you the 90 or 45 choice.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:18.160
I personally could not handle the 45.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:23.260
I did try both and the 90 just seemed much more intuitive for my kind of play.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:24.880
Yeah, I'm the same way there.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:37.660
I looked it up in the manual and they say that you're supposed to kind of hold your controller like a remote and that's how you do the 45 degree grip.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.500
It's a special technique.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:41.440
I'm not really sure what the deal is with that.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:53.880
Yeah, I saw that too, like holding your controller at a 45 degree angle, which seems like in principle, or not in principle, but in theory, seems like it would work well.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:09:07.100
But I feel like that's just even more confusing because even if you're holding it at a 45 degree angle, you just automatically want to treat the up button like the up button and not like the northeast button.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.340
Yeah, yeah.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:19.960
I don't really get that either because like if you treat it like that, that's sort of just like saying like turn your TV upside down and also hold your controller upside down and it'll still work.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.160
And so that's sort of like how I was like, why is this even an option?

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:28.880
But I guess some people wanted it or are used to it or I don't know.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:36.200
Well, correct me if I'm wrong, but the original controller for the arcade version was a ball that you would move around, right?

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:37.420
Yeah, it was a track ball.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.700
Yeah. So I guess it's obviously like the best option.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.700
Yeah, I guess they just like didn't know what to do to simulate that.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.520
They're like, I guess you could hold it like a remote and then that's like a ball, right?

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:49.640
And people are like, yeah, sure.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:55.300
It's the 80s. We did see this on Q-Bert though, me and Sean, when we did that episode.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:10:00.760
Q-Bert also suggested like, you know, tilt the controller a little bit different.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:04.920
So this way you can play with understanding the cubes better.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:07.340
I think we both ruled it out there too, right, Sean?

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:20.040
Oh yeah. And it's also interesting that recently we've just been getting a lot of games that have some weird controller or like control issues like just coming from the arcade.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.140
Like between this and quarterback and Q-Bert.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:31.340
We're getting a lot of things that aren't supposed to be on D-pads now on D-pads.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:36.620
Well, that's because the NES has established itself as the premier home console.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.900
So if your arcade game's worth anything, it would also be available on the NES.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.300
That's true. All right.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.600
Back to the marble for a second with the movement.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:50.140
We talked about that there is a time limit to each of these stages.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.020
Does the marble feel like it is speed?

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.220
Like do you feel fast using the marble?

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.240
We mentioned that there's momentum and stuff.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.300
Do you, you know, does that hold it back?

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:03.220
Does that help it? Did you wish the marble was faster, Sam?

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.740
I think that I like the speed of the marble.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:23.080
I found that if I try to hurry through stages, I usually mess up and that it's better to just take it slowly and carefully and then get to know the stages a lot better that way than just trying to go down every path as fast as I can.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:29.240
So, and if I took it carefully, I was able to get through stages pretty easily.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.460
So, yeah. Yeah.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:35.720
That was the, that was the thing that took me a little while to, to get my head around.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:46.700
Cause when you first play a game that's, you know, about a marble like this on a marble coaster, you're thinking speed, you know, you're thinking like you want that momentum, but the game's not really built around that idea.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:49.900
The game's more built around like, I don't know, I feel like the game is meant to be played.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:58.960
Like you're supposed to be a little more cerebral about it and like think your way through and like the momentum can help you try and get through the course, but also it can, it can be an obstacle.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:08.140
Like, because there's, you know, the course is built with so many like very small, narrow ledges and whatnot.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.640
It takes some time, some time to kind of start thinking of it that way.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:19.500
Joe, I think that's a great point because there's to the cerebral nature of it.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.360
I remember thinking that even just the penalty for falling off the stage was too forgiving.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.220
This is the, this is like a short burst of stages.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:33.840
I kind of feel like if you fall off a stage like that, it should be Super Mario Brothers level of consequences.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:38.720
Like that you got to go back to the beginning of the stage or you got, you know, like they're, they're not really like that.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:44.640
Sort of surprised me. Like I would, I would agree that you're supposed to sort of take this carefully and be a bit more precise.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:53.740
But when it really just warps you back like a few inches and it, you get control of your ball again pretty quickly.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:57.060
Like you can kind of, you can kind of come at this haphazardly.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:04.720
I know that like in terms of score, I think you do get some penalties, but it's, it's, it's still very small penalty.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:10.840
So I think that does allow you to, to try and get a little loose though.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:19.460
Like it's not, because, because there's so few levels like, and I would inevitably sort of run at a time by the end.

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:21.940
And I played the first level pretty often.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:28.440
And by the end, by like my last playthrough, I could just sort of very quickly glide through it.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:29.760
And that does feel good.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:34.240
It's just like a very short burst of that good feeling though.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.340
I actually thought that the, like how forgiving it was.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:45.260
I was kind of surprised, but in a good way that they were like willing to make it so forgiving.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:49.540
Cause it was kind of in an age where nothing was that forgiving on the NES, you know?

00:13:49.740 --> 00:14:00.120
And, and I do think that I do agree that it, it makes you maybe not think quite as carefully as you would if you like got sent back to the beginning of the stage or something when you fell off.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:04.260
But I also think that, that it was a good choice to not do that.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.820
I don't know. I feel like, I feel like that would be too much.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.620
Like, I feel like the punishment is that you lose time every time you fall off.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:18.600
You lose a couple of seconds and while that might seem small, since your seconds all add up over the whole course of your playthrough.

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:24.720
Like once you get to level six, if you screwed up seven times in level one, that might be the nail in your coffin.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:28.740
Like, yeah, we won't even be able to finish it if you'd have a perfect run.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:34.260
And maybe this is just personal preference, but I feel like if I, if I were to fall off, every time I fell off, I went back to the beginning of stage.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.200
I don't think I would enjoy this game very much.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:40.500
You don't think it would have taught you to play more cautiously though?

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:53.760
I think it would have definitely taught me to play more cautiously, but I think that that would have been, I mean, again, this is like a preference thing, but I think that that's, that's across the line of what I, between like enjoyable and, and like.

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.440
Egregious. And yeah, yeah.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:03.760
Like there's, there's a level and there's a lot of things where it's like, you know, if you talk about like dark souls being punishing because it's punishing because you want to like learn.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:10.200
But with this, with, I feel like it would just be, there'd be more of a barrier to entry, you know?

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.020
Like I would just be like, okay, like I'm playing as a marble.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.660
I die every time. I don't get anywhere.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:21.420
That would take me so long to get to the next level that it'd be like, is, is it enough to keep me trying?

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:22.760
Like, okay, I get it. I'm a marble.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:28.060
You know what I mean? Like after a while, if it's so difficult, I'd probably, probably put it down.

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:45.640
And I, and I actually had the opposite effect giving me one thing that I think under, undercuts either play style, whether you're trying to like go for speed or go for precision and thinking more carefully about it is just the level geometry and the perspective.

00:15:46.580 --> 00:16:08.100
I don't know about you guys, but there were a lot of situations that I feel like the, whether it's just like the, if you want to call it like hit boxes of the levels or just like the moments on the stage where it signals you're falling off now.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:14.340
Uh, it felt a little, like, uh, it felt a little weird to me and not like one-to-one.

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.980
I think maybe there should, there should have been a bit more wiggle room if you could like sort of hop a ledge a little bit.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:24.240
Um, because I just fell in places that I didn't think I'd fall.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:31.260
I'm all for, you know, being punished for, uh, miscalculating my speed and going barreling off of a ledge.

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:42.140
But when it's, when there are so many situations where I didn't think that that would have happened and then it did, uh, it's not as enjoyable of an experience.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:54.920
Yeah. To roll off that idea, uh, it's, it's also, it's also kind of interesting how easily your marble will shatter, uh, in certain situations.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:17:06.580
Like sometimes the marble's cool with the shortcut and you're fine, but, you know, if the game knows that you're kind of doing something it doesn't want you to do, like skipping a ledge or something like that,

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:12.040
the ball just cracks in half and eats up a good amount of time and then you have to wait for it to reanimate back on too.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:13.600
I was a little surprised by that.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:15.520
I thought the game was encouraging shortcuts.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:19.960
I mean, some of the layouts have it built in where you can choose your own path and stuff.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:25.880
I was a little surprised to see that they didn't want you to like clip down if, if it was possible.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:30.080
I'm not talking about jumping over in, you know, gaps or anything like that.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.240
I'm talking about like even rolling down ledges.

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:37.560
If you can see the ground below you, I think that's fair game to force your marble to roll off into that.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:43.840
Yeah. They definitely have to redesign these levels then because the game would be trivially easy.

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:52.460
I feel like in a lot of situations, it gets a lot of, it gets a lot of its difficulty out of the fact that you kind of see where you're going.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.000
But if you were to take that shortcut, you'd shatter.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:05.880
But no, I think in terms of like the game feel, I was, it was very unintuitive how little wiggle room you had for fall damage.

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:15.900
It was kind of like that, that, that, this spelunker, like where you kind of jump half a foot and then your legs crunch from under you.

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:17.680
It was that sort of feel to me.

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:21.640
I had the same, the same feeling at first too.

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:33.520
And then I kind of started to treat it as like, okay, well then the game is to just stay flat on the ground or, you know, flat or on an incline or whatever, but to not like leave the ground because then you, then you'll break and then you lose time.

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:39.320
But then there were a couple of times where part of the course was going off a ledge.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:52.860
Yeah. Where then I was, then it did feel a little bit troublesome because it was, it was like they had times where they, you know, they intended you to jump and times where they don't.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.960
And sometimes it wasn't that easy to like tell those apart.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:57.520
Yeah, I agree completely.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:07.360
There isn't enough like internal rule following for it to feel all that quite right.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:14.540
But after a couple of playthroughs, like you, you, you end up learning, but I think that's sort of like a lazy way to approach it.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:24.440
The momentum thing also matters there too, though, because there's some where you can only reach those gaps if you have a good amount of speed built in too.

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:36.540
And that's, that's a punishing thing to find out as well, that you have to kind of go back a little bit and recreate your momentum and dodge the obstacles in order to clear certain paths.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:05.240
So these layouts with the, you know, the varying, the difficulty definitely shifts.

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:10.180
The first level I think is designed just to, you know, give you a feel of how to control the marble.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.280
Then they introduce an obstacle here or there.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:16.240
And then they introduce different ideas of how to get through a course.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:22.820
I personally found the difficulty in the later, I think there's, is there five or six levels?

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:43.800
I found the difficulty in the later, like, yeah, the last like three levels have things in them that are also just so different than the beginning parts of the game that I struggle to think of how you're supposed to, you know, like uncover the idea of how to, how to conquer this stuff when you're only seeing it in glimpses, you know, because of your time.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:49.440
So you have to like keep getting back to them and then relearn them when you finally get back to that part.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:52.800
I didn't enjoy that aspect of it.

00:20:52.840 --> 00:21:03.600
I thought they could have taught you everything that the game needed you to know about beating it throughout the entirety of the game rather than introducing it in a particular stage at its most challenging point.

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:17.580
Yeah. The only time that you see a platform sort of move or kind of disappear and reappear is the literal like last stretch of the entire game.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:24.720
And at first glance, you get to that jump or what looks like a jump.

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:35.560
You think it's just a jump, but no, you have to slow the hell down and wait for the platform below you to materialize and then sort of try and stick with that.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:42.340
It didn't really have any, like, it's not like there were mechanics that built off of each other.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:47.760
It was just over the course of this game, there'd be an idea and then it would disappear.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.300
And then there'd be another idea and then you wouldn't see it again.

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:59.200
So I thought that that was, it just wasn't very, it didn't have a consistency to it.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:03.240
I, I'm willing to forgive it though, because there are only six levels.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:16.760
I think that the fact that they're so different from each other and that they have all these different, uh, all these different ideas that kind of come out of nowhere and catch you off guard.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.780
I think that helps elongate the game.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:29.180
And I don't think it does it in a cheap way that some games do, because I feel like it is kind of easy to get back to that point.

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:31.920
After you find out this new thing that stumps you.

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:35.620
And if you get there once, then you can get there again.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:42.080
Okay. So yes, the, the shortness of the game, like the six stages, I'm not asking them to build any more stages.

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:49.560
Like I think that actually, believe it or not, is appropriate because people who are amazing at this game will just continue to try to beat it faster and faster.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:54.920
And people who, uh, can't master a certain stage can get back to it relatively quickly.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:23:05.480
I think what I'm asking for is, you know, the designers to take a look at, you know, Super Mario Brothers level of teaching you how to do something.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:06.760
So to Sean's point, right?

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:14.020
The invisible, uh, or the teleporting platforms that you learn at the very last part of the game.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:23.240
That level could have had more of that just in a way where it's like, you know, one platform reappears and disappears first and you clear that section.

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:27.920
And then it's like, you know, uh, three of them like appear on and off.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:36.740
And then finally you get to the one where it like staggers and you, you know, by that point it's up to you to take what you learned from the first two obstacles and bring it into the third.

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:42.500
I'm a little surprised that the game only like really offers these things immediately at their most challenging.

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.060
I think that's the argument I'm making.

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:45.720
I get that.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:53.000
I think that for that, I think that this game does need more levels because it's just such a short experience.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:24:04.900
Like I was able to not, I didn't actually, um, do this all in one sitting, but like over a short amount of playing period, like I did beat the game.

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:10.600
I know that you're supposed to be able to beat the game pretty easily and it's all about like getting a higher score and having better times.

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:14.540
But I just didn't think that there's enough content here.

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:32.440
I think that like in able to like sort of implement what you're saying, Mike, you would need sort of sub levels or maybe like some, some levels between all of these levels, uh, to have like that, like lower stakes version of teaching mechanics.

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:36.340
And I think that the game would just have more value that way too.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:41.980
Um, because I, I, these, it's like a six minute, 10 minute game.

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:44.540
If you were to just like complete it outright.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:56.520
So, well, even if you pretend, if you're not even the best kind of player, right, it's still only a 10 minute game because that's how like, that's the max amount of time they give you to beat the entire game.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:25:04.460
So, even if you're not great at the games, I think what's funny is, is that it had the reverse effect on me where I was playing the game pretty fast at first.

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:06.060
Like I was like, yeah, I own this marble.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:10.220
And then I kept dying in the fourth stage, I want to say.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:17.200
So, I looked up the game on YouTube to see some things and I saw what the final stage was and I was like, well, I'll never beat that.

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:21.680
So, I might as well take the rest of the game pretty slow and not care about defeating the game.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:28.580
So, I agree that, that it can only be a positive thing to add more levels and I, and I think that that, you know, that, that would be an improvement.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:39.180
But I, it didn't bother me so much that like, like, all right, so the example that you guys keep using about the, those, those platforms that are appearing at the end of the level.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:45.460
Yeah, when you first get there, it probably would kind of suck when you first get there and, and that's what screws you because you didn't know it was coming.

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:49.980
But, but I will say pretty quickly, you see what's happening and you understand what you're supposed to do.

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:53.980
It's, it's mastering that ability that, that might take some time.

00:25:54.080 --> 00:26:00.360
But, you know, I, I kind of see these levels each as like their own little level to master.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:14.580
And I think that one other thing that maybe we are not looking at, just because of the nature of the way we do this podcast, is that I think that this game shines mostly as a two-player game.

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:20.660
Like, I think that that's where, where this game is like, has, has the most value.

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:22.000
It makes sense because of the race, they can.

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:27.780
Racing somebody else. So it's like, you come up to this obstacle and one of you's got to master it before the other one does.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:32.340
And it's just like a frantic race to try and, try and beat this obstacle.

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:40.120
So like I said, I do think it would be better to have more levels, but I think that just having, like, kind of like Sam said, these six levels that are each very unique from each other.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:43.720
And it's like, okay, this time I'm going to try and master the, like, reverse gravity level.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:50.820
I mean, I'm saying that fully aware that, like, the game doesn't let you just select whichever level you want to jump into.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.120
So you do have to, like, get there each time.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:58.360
But still, I think that it, I still think it works.

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:00.780
Were you able to play this two-player?

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:02.420
I wasn't.

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:06.480
So I'm kind of talking, like, I'm kind of talking out of, like, just my baseless opinion.

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:08.860
No, usually you do find a way.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:14.060
But I agree that I would have more fun two-player probably.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:22.920
But, yeah, I think that that's going to have to be the next time I approach this game is when someone plays with me.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:33.220
But going back to the six stages, I think, you know, having that in the home console version is understandable for the time period.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.920
But we're also talking about this game in 2021.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:40.720
And it's not really, like, forgivable anymore if we're reviewing it today.

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:48.940
This home console version should have absolutely had, like, an arcade mode where you play these six and it's a race for time.

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:55.760
And then, like, you know, a hundred levels of various Marble Madness layouts.

00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:59.600
Because, you know, I'm not a game designer by any means.

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:03.720
But I could imagine there's a lot more that you could add to this kind of concept.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.800
And it's shocking that they never did.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.380
Like, these six levels are Marble Madness always.

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.460
Like, there's never more, never less.

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:16.580
Yeah, it's not like this is just, like, no, we're only going to use the purest designs.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.440
And anything more would just be fluff.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:25.440
Like, no, there's a lot of interesting things you can do with these trial runs.

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:33.420
You could double or, yeah, add a hundred levels here and it wouldn't feel flat.

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:39.560
Right. And so I think that's what the home console version needed because you could even, at that point, you could remove the time limit part.

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:43.560
And just make it about navigating your Marble throughout those stages.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:53.220
And, you know, you could get even crazier with the amounts of enemies on screen or the narrowness of the passageways.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:58.120
I'm sure that in another universe that exists.

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:02.740
But for some reason, Marble Madness is the only game in its franchise.

00:29:03.820 --> 00:29:04.860
My God.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:06.440
Doesn't seem right.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:08.240
We're in the wrong timeline.

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:39.700
Ha! Which brings me to my next point.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.100
What if there was no time limit in this game?

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.260
Specifically for the home console version, too.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:47.500
Like, I get it in the arcade.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:48.800
You want to eat people's quarters.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:56.800
We've been there. For the home console version, if there was a version of this game where you play the same exact layouts and everything.

00:29:56.840 --> 00:30:01.340
But now it's like a no time limit, easy mode of the game.

00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:08.840
Would you have welcomed that just to have another way to experience the game and take your time and learn the mechanics of the later stages?

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:17.880
I think because the game is so short that if you take out the time limit, you take out really the challenge.

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:20.120
It needs to have some kind of pressure.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:24.500
Whether it's a time limit or lives or some other thing.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:29.920
You can't just sort of have it be a free roam racer.

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:31.340
I don't know. Yeah.

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:36.620
Lives would be an interesting alternative, though, because then that would give you the time to, like, really take your timer on a corner.

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:39.480
I mean, maybe that would also be a very different experience.

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:41.040
Yeah. Definitely, yeah.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:42.300
Maybe there's, like, a mode.

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:44.480
There's timed mode and there's lives mode, you know?

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:46.240
Yeah. Absolutely.

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:49.840
And what about more tricks, too?

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:55.000
And by that I mean, like, shortcuts, passageways.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:58.520
You know, we're kind of designing new layouts.

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:00.980
We can't really think about ways we would add to the current game.

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:11.780
But, like, I think of this, especially even from the multiplayer side of it, too, you want to find the best way to race to the finish.

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:17.100
And I feel like sometimes there's only one path, and that's not really all that interesting.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:22.620
Yeah. I mean, I think that that's another thing that goes with what we all keep suggesting is having more levels.

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:40.200
I think this is just, this just inherently has the potential for so many different ideas to be implemented in so many different, like, crazy layouts and weird tricks and weird, you know, obstacles and stuff that, yeah, I think it would definitely benefit from, like, tons of creativity on that level.

00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:46.640
And how about that music?

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:47.980
Music is dope.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.080
It's great music in this game.

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:53.660
I would welcome more music.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:32:03.260
I feel like if you beat the last, like, the last level, then you should go back to the first one and have, like, new music when you play it or something.

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:06.400
Or, hey, new levels means new music.

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.120
You get the best of both worlds there.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:13.160
That's true. I'm telling these designers.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:20.120
I like that, though, Sam. The idea that, like, if you beat it, you get to go back and there's, like, crazier sound effects and stuff.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:21.680
A different aesthetic to the game.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:28.100
Like the Mock Rider kind of treatment where you get that new song unlocked after you make it to however many levels.

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:31.600
Yeah, that's a, and that's, like, it's not even a new song.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:35.260
It's, like, a slight adjustment to a part of the original song.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.160
So, like, yeah, I totally agree with that.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:40.360
The music in the NES version is really cool.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:51.480
And I think David Weiss, who's the rare composer guy who makes all your favorite rare game music, Diddy Kong Racing, Banjo-Kazooie, all that.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.200
He worked on the NES versions.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:33:06.100
But the arcade version where this thing all started, that version is somehow even trippier and, like, more out there in terms of the music and of the aesthetic of the game.

00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:13.320
And just, like, the idea of, like, a marble in a, you know, in a CG world in space.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:22.660
Like, there's something very, that Treehouse of Horror 6, I think it is, where Homer goes to the CG world that he finds in his living room.

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:24.140
Oh, yeah. It's like that, you know?

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:29.120
Like, there's just something, it feels wrong to be in the marble madness world, you know?

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:34.380
Like, we don't belong there and we're intruding on the marble's space.

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:37.220
That's why those little green lampreys are trying to get us.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:44.560
So you feel, like, an intense anxiety when you hear that music with this game.

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:51.820
There's, yeah, it's definitely, like, Windows 95, it's now safe to turn off your computer box.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.160
Yeah, I know exactly what you mean.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:55.100
That's perfect.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:34:01.340
But it's funny, though, because I was going to say that some of the tracks in the NES version, like, give me this anxiety.

00:34:01.340 --> 00:34:10.140
I think it's the third level has, like, a very, like, rapid and, like, I don't know, anxious, like, circus-sounding soundtrack.

00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:16.340
And that makes it, like, you know, it's kind of cheating to say this because I didn't play two players.

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:22.120
But the whole time I was kind of treating it like I'm practicing for when I actually play against somebody else.

00:34:23.220 --> 00:34:29.640
And it seems like, you know, it's a frantic race where it's like you've got to, you're balancing, like, how fast you want to go with how careful you want to be.

00:34:29.640 --> 00:34:34.780
And, like, that music kind of, I feel like, makes, adds to that, like, tension and anxiety.

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:45.980
And in the fourth level also where you get this, like, you get this, like, what sounds like a demonic, like, boss's dungeon in, like, any other sort of, like, platformer.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:48.920
And, you know, all the platforms are, like, red and everything.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:59.500
And it just feels like, I don't know, I'm just imagining racing someone and being, like, your heart racing because you're, like, trying to balance that caution with, like,

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:01.300
what's the opposite of caution?

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:02.700
I forget. Safety?

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:03.880
No. Rashness.

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:05.460
What's the... It would be, yeah.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:13.440
I'm getting the vibe that we're all just very anxious people and my mental illness related to marbles is not the only one.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:17.520
I wouldn't say that I'm anxious while playing this game.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:20.740
I think it just, it creates an interesting environment.

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:23.780
And, yes, Joe, like, the NES version definitely has that.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:29.360
I was just giving the arcade a moment there because it definitely, like, is that times ten.

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:31.940
Like, I think it's the fifth stage.

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:37.940
There's something about it where you're now, you start at the bottom and you have to climb, like, up the mountain.

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:42.160
And that also feels wrong even though I've only been playing this game for six minutes.

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:44.960
All of a sudden I'm like, wait, now we're going the other way?

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:46.100
Like, what is happening?

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:49.080
You know, like, there's so much...

00:35:51.720 --> 00:36:02.660
It's not dread, you know, but there's so much confusion going on in Marvel Madness in the sense of purpose and being and abstractness that I'm totally here for it.

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:12.940
Like, I love that about it and I actually couldn't, even though I could never get close to beating the game, I couldn't put it down because of this feeling it gives.

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:29.340
Yeah, and well, to add to what you're talking about with that level that's going up, just so the listeners know, too, that that level is particularly unsettling because not only are you going in the opposite direction as normal, but gravity on the inclines and declines is inverted.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:38.140
So you, like, roll up inclines as if you're rolling down a hill, which really just makes you have to, like, think about...

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:44.020
It's like when the left and right control is inverted in a game and you've got to think about, wait, am I going to go left or am I going to go right when I press this?

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:46.940
It's, like, it's very confusing.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:51.020
And in such a... Now, you could just turn your TV upside down and then everything would be fine.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:56.000
But gravity when you're falling or on a flat plane still works normally, so that would still be confusing.

00:36:57.200 --> 00:37:05.400
And to the listeners at home, you could have finished this game maybe three or four times in the time that we've been talking about it.

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:07.860
If you're good at it.

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:10.900
This took me, like, a couple hours to get to that.

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:15.460
I didn't even beat it. I got to the last level, but I could not beat that last part.

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:28.280
So one thing that I thought was a little weird was the little magic wand that comes after your guy that resurrects you.

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:36.520
Or I had one case where I was at the top of a hill and I couldn't go any further and my marble stopped.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:41.540
And then a magic wand came and tapped me and I got a little extra boost to make it to the top of the hill.

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:51.540
Did anyone else have any weird magic wand experiences or want to kind of, like, deconstruct this weird magic wand?

00:37:51.740 --> 00:37:54.020
I thought that, like, gave you time.

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:55.560
I really don't know.

00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.120
Like, it would randomly do it.

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:02.840
I thought that it was sort of in the same vein as, like, the...

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:09.040
When you crush yourself accidentally, it'll come and sweep you up.

00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:13.100
But I don't really know what it was doing half the time.

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:14.160
I just let it happen.

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:17.420
Yeah, I only ever had it grant me an extra 10 seconds.

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:19.480
But I have no idea what triggered it.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:24.160
It feels like it happens when you're, like, steering the ball really well.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:29.380
Because it was always appearing when nothing else was really going on.

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:31.440
It just kind of halted my gameplay for a second.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.380
And I'm like, huh, what was that about?

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.420
Like, I couldn't quite tell if I'd done anything right.

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.740
But I definitely wasn't, you know...

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:41.240
I was being cautious during those moments.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:42.820
So maybe it's, like, a reward.

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:50.020
Now, you reminded me, too, Sam, that, like, other than, like, adding time and stuff,

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:54.660
there is another thing to do in this game other than push around the D-pad.

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:57.220
And that is the turbocharging.

00:38:57.860 --> 00:39:01.880
Pressing the A button gives you a little extra burst of speed.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:05.400
Did anybody actually experience that extra burst of speed and use it to their advantage?

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:10.360
I tried quite a bit, but I could not notice any burst of speed.

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:13.760
I mean, do you need to, like, collect points before you can use it or something?

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:15.440
No. And I tried a lot to use that.

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:24.500
No, there were a couple times that, like, I don't know, I approached either a jump or an incline with, like,

00:39:24.500 --> 00:39:27.940
it just wasn't enough momentum to clear it.

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:38.140
But then if you just return and then hold the, whichever button, hold the turbo button down, you can...

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:39.620
Like, it does make you faster.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:42.120
It was clear to me, at least.

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:49.980
Just... Okay, because I did use it a couple times to, like, try and jump, but I could never tell, like, did I use it or did I just happen to get lucky that time?

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:51.480
Like, it was never noticeable to me.

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:56.720
But there was, like, one or two times where I jumped from one ledge to another, a jump that I'd been missing, and I made it.

00:39:56.920 --> 00:40:01.520
But visibly, it didn't look like anything changed when I pressed A, so I wasn't sure if it was just coincidence.

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:05.300
Yeah, it definitely gives you a bit of a...

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:07.460
a bit more power there.

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:16.540
It is a... it's a dangerous thing, though, too, because sometimes, depending on if you're going down a ramp or if you're in a narrow passageway,

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:23.460
like, it's already pretty hard and easy to lose control of your ball and then to also be, like, turbocharging.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:29.180
I imagine that for speedruns, you're just constantly mashing the A button as you're going through the level.

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:35.120
You definitely got to decelerate when, like, immediately after using whatever...

00:40:35.120 --> 00:40:38.080
using that to clear whatever obstacle you're trying to get by.

00:40:38.900 --> 00:40:41.500
But that's a really satisfying part of the game, too, right?

00:40:41.640 --> 00:40:48.320
Like, there's something where you're going really fast and then you have to make a turn and you're, like, pumping the brakes.

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:54.100
You know, whatever direction you're going in, you're holding the two buttons on the D-pad that, like, break in the opposite direction.

00:40:54.100 --> 00:41:00.920
And it's really satisfying to watch the ball, like, halt and not fall off the cliff and instead just inch its way back over.

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:01.960
I really like that part.

00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:12.260
Yeah. And I think that's where it derives most of, like, the enjoyment of playing it is just getting that down and getting that flow.

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:13.220
So, yeah.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:21.600
An interesting thing I thought I found about, like, those physics is that it did feel, like, very, very realistic.

00:41:21.860 --> 00:41:27.380
Like, the way, you know, if you're inching towards a ledge and, like, coming to a halt has, like...

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:29.760
You don't come to a direct halt immediately enough.

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:34.040
You know, you still have that momentum going and it feels realistic.

00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:45.660
But the funny thing is when you really think about it, unless you're going down a decline or going up an incline, if you're on a flat plane, like, those physics don't really translate to anything in the real world.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:50.480
Because your motion is coming from you pushing the marble in some way or controlling the marble.

00:41:50.980 --> 00:41:51.840
Do you know what I mean?

00:41:52.220 --> 00:41:54.280
Like, it's...

00:41:55.220 --> 00:42:03.420
Like, I'll be on a ledge and being, like, inching closer and it feels like, yeah, I can't just, like, with a character that takes a step, take one step closer.

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:08.800
I press it and it rolls a little bit and it feels like a realistic amount of roll for, like, one...

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:10.200
Or for the amount of time I'm pressing it.

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:14.000
But I don't know what that actually means, a realistic amount of roll for what I'm...

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:14.800
That doesn't...

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:16.900
In real world, I'm not controlling a marble.

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:18.680
That's only Sean. But...

00:42:19.180 --> 00:42:22.240
Do you know what I mean? Like, I don't know if I'm even articulating that right, but it's like...

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:23.360
I get what you're saying. It's...

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:24.500
Because it's a physics simulation.

00:42:24.620 --> 00:42:26.220
So you're adding a little bit of...

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:27.660
Just a tiny bit of force to it.

00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:28.980
Yeah. And then... It's well calibrated.

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:33.400
Yeah. Like, the more force that you add, like, the longer you hold down a button or a...

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:35.280
The more force is added.

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.040
And it keeps adding force.

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:38.360
And that's what gives you momentum.

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:42.960
Yeah. I guess I was just, like, impressed with how intuitive it ended up feeling.

00:42:43.320 --> 00:42:46.920
When, like, they were kind of starting from scratch.

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:53.520
You know, it wasn't like they had to use the physics of, like, what a marble would do on a 45 degree angle.

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:56.340
They had to just, like, make up, like, okay, how much pressure...

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:57.780
How much force are you putting on the marble?

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:39.320
We'll be right back. The creator of Marble Madness is also the creator of gaming icon Knack from Knack and Knack 2.

00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:40.580
The Knack?

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:46.820
That is Mark Cerny, who's now a pretty big deal at PlayStation, right?

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:49.940
I feel like he works on everything that they do.

00:43:50.980 --> 00:43:54.020
At least has a thank you in the credits.

00:43:54.020 --> 00:43:58.340
As recently as even that new Miles Morales Spider-Man game.

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:06.420
I don't even know how you could be involved in something like that and also be busy working on Knack 3 at the same time.

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:07.820
I don't think he should.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:14.100
He shouldn't be splitting his time on anything else but Knack 3 to make it the best game it can possibly be.

00:44:14.100 --> 00:44:21.800
But for real, Mark Cerny's credit list is really underappreciated and we were just kind of going over it before the show.

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:30.260
But I mean the guy is like Crash Bandicoot, Spyro, Ratchet & Clank, Uncharted.

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:33.280
Like all these gigantic PlayStation games.

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:36.120
But he started here with Marble Madness.

00:44:36.400 --> 00:44:42.860
And in fact before Marble Madness, I think he was trying to make a video game adaptation of Michael Jackson's Thriller.

00:44:44.180 --> 00:44:46.620
And that just didn't work out.

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:47.560
So he was like, oh, you know what?

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:48.960
I'm also into marbles.

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:53.120
So he made the original Marble Madness, the arcade version.

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:56.220
Yes, he made the arcade version of Marble Madness.

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:59.400
And other people worked on it with him, of course.

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:01.540
But he was the game designer.

00:45:01.980 --> 00:45:06.180
He had a job at Atari and pitched a few different ideas at first.

00:45:06.200 --> 00:45:12.740
I think a lot of it had to do with like either miniature golf or inspired by miniature golf style controls.

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:17.420
And Atari was like, nah, we don't really want to do that.

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:20.700
And he goes, okay, well what if I do miniature golf with racing?

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.300
And they were like, tell us more.

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:25.060
And somehow Marble Madness came out of that.

00:45:25.200 --> 00:45:27.480
I'm sure I should have read some more of the book.

00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:29.780
But that's the gist of what I got.

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:36.840
So from that, I think once the game's objective became more around a race theme and a race against time specifically,

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:40.720
the idea of what you could do with a marble became Marble Madness.

00:45:40.740 --> 00:45:41.620
And it was in the arcades.

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:43.240
And it was a huge hit actually.

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:49.640
But now we think of – I don't think anybody thinks of Marble Madness

00:45:49.640 --> 00:45:50.980
and Mark Cerny together.

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:52.540
But like this was his –

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:55.580
I don't really think about Marble Madness that much.

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:59.120
Well, I also don't think people think about Mark Cerny that much either, right?

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:02.680
Like video gamers know who Shigeru Miyamoto is.

00:46:02.820 --> 00:46:05.480
I wouldn't say every video gamer knows who Mark Cerny is.

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:07.120
I didn't until today.

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:10.840
Okay, then I guess you're proving me wrong right away before I even said my point.

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:12.820
Because I was going to say I thought we all would have known.

00:46:13.640 --> 00:46:17.340
Never heard of him. Like I don't know too much about his resume.

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:19.780
But when you said Mark Cerny, I was like, oh, yeah, Mark Cerny.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:22.020
You know, the PlayStation guy is what I think of him as.

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:26.080
Right, right. Maybe just because I just remember hearing him from E3s.

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:30.380
And I played a lot of Ratchet & Clank and Crash Bandicoot and Spyro and all those games.

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:37.960
So why didn't Cerny go on to make Marble Man Marble Madness 2?

00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:40.780
So, wait, say that again?

00:46:41.540 --> 00:46:46.920
Like, why would it, if you're Mark Cerny, right, and you just made Marble Madness and it's a hit and everything like that,

00:46:47.080 --> 00:46:51.020
why wouldn't you go on to make Marble Man Marble Madness 2?

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.580
Why is it called Marble Man?

00:46:55.740 --> 00:47:03.980
Well, because Marble Man Marble Madness 2 was the arcade sequel that was planned for release in 1991.

00:47:04.780 --> 00:47:12.060
And although Mark Cerny was uninvolved in development, the game did get a prototype release in several arcades.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:14.680
And there is video footage of this game in action.

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:17.000
It just was never officially released.

00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:24.120
So my question stands, why wouldn't Mark Cerny want to be involved in Marble Man Marble Madness 2?

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:27.560
I think that the whole situation was just too personal to him.

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:32.660
And to see it turned into this capitalist scheme to just make more money,

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:36.940
like, I wouldn't want to see that happen to my baby Marble Madness either.

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:44.400
You either die a Marble Madness or you live long enough to become Marble Man 2 or whatever it was.

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:47.660
No, I think that, yeah.

00:47:47.660 --> 00:47:51.340
Marble Man does sound like the existential crisis that Sean is having.

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:53.040
I've got Marble Man Syndrome.

00:47:54.860 --> 00:48:03.020
I feel like it's like, yeah, Marble Madness is a game that is just exactly what it wants to be.

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:05.560
It wants to be a game where marbles roll down these courses.

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:08.800
You don't see somebody inventing football 2.

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:10.160
It's just football.

00:48:10.340 --> 00:48:11.720
And there's no hockey 2.

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:12.720
It's like a sport.

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:17.760
He wants those players to spend their time mastering it.

00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:19.700
Marble Madness 92.

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.460
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:25.460
You just get new marbles to play as, but it's all the same courses.

00:48:25.820 --> 00:48:31.100
Marble Man, Marble Madness 2 did have a couple of extra features, though, okay?

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:34.660
Joe, you were excited about the multiplayer in Marble Madness.

00:48:34.920 --> 00:48:41.620
Imagine your excitement when they add a whole other player to the mix, up to three players in Marble Madness 2.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:44.520
Wow. I love it.

00:48:45.020 --> 00:48:46.400
Why couldn't they just do four?

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:47.860
No, that's ridiculous.

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:49.640
Yeah, I wouldn't do that at all.

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:59.200
There was also going to be a pinball minigame in between levels, where I imagine you control the marble and not the actual pinball machine.

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:01.080
That's a cool idea.

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:05.160
I like that. There was going to be power-ups that your marble could get.

00:49:05.280 --> 00:49:09.600
That almost gives me some, I know it's different, but it almost gives me some Kirby's Dream Course vibes.

00:49:10.700 --> 00:49:11.800
Yeah, that's true.

00:49:11.960 --> 00:49:19.980
That's true. I think the power-ups were going to include stuff like invincibility or invisibility against other players.

00:49:20.760 --> 00:49:25.660
And apparently flight was going to be another power-up that you could fly with your marble.

00:49:25.880 --> 00:49:27.820
Wait, invisibility or invincibility?

00:49:27.820 --> 00:49:34.260
Invincibility. See, I said invincibility, but I think it's invisibility, which doesn't sound like a power-up.

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:36.200
How could you do that? Oh, this is the arcade.

00:49:36.540 --> 00:49:37.660
Are there two screens?

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:40.240
Like, how could you do that?

00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:41.540
No, no, the marble would just be gone.

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:43.340
Oh, so you can't see it either?

00:49:43.460 --> 00:49:44.900
You can't see it either? You're the one controlling it?

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:52.680
Correct. So the other person gets the power-up and then you turn invisible.

00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:55.640
It's a punishment that they set on you, I'm assuming.

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:57.020
Right, I suppose. I suppose.

00:49:57.020 --> 00:50:04.540
I never played it because it never came out because it didn't perform well up against games like Street Fighter 2.

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:13.160
So you could understand that, like, they were like, hmm, this niche marble game isn't really competing with the genre-defining fighting game.

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:16.180
I don't know why I'm just cracking up right now.

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:21.200
We got, like, really set on that invisibility thing.

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:23.380
Well, I was so confused on that.

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:33.820
So because it didn't take off, the arcade system boards for the sequel were rumored to have been destroyed to clear inventory for tax purposes.

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:36.480
Something about that sentence doesn't sit well with me.

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:39.480
I don't think tax purposes are required.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:45.020
Make sure you burn those boards before the tax people find out.

00:50:45.020 --> 00:51:03.000
No, I think that Mark Cerny actually got his justice and convinced the CEO to destroy them and not to diminish his labor of love with this piece of garbage sequel.

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:08.700
Yeah, I imagine it kind of like a seal door in Lord of the Rings, like, trying to throw the ring in.

00:51:08.820 --> 00:51:11.480
And Mark Cerny is, like, behind him saying, like, throw it in.

00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:14.040
We have to destroy it.

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:23.680
I understand. I think what wound up happening was at the time, Cerny wanted to work on Marble Madness 2, but he had huge gambling debts.

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:30.960
And at the time, you know, Atari was like, you've got to leave the company for a little bit while you have these gigantic gambling debts.

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:40.200
Travel to Japan, go work with Sega, make Sonic the Hedgehog 2 for, like, a year, and then come back and work for us when the gambling debts are cleared.

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:41.700
And that's exactly what he went to do.

00:51:41.900 --> 00:51:44.060
So that's why he didn't work on Marble Madness 2.

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:45.860
Is this, is that real?

00:51:45.860 --> 00:51:46.820
I thought you just made that up.

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:50.900
Well, so he did work on Sonic the Hedgehog 2.

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:52.900
No gambling debts.

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:54.280
That we know of.

00:51:54.280 --> 00:52:02.660
No gambling debts, but true believers will know exactly who I'm actually referencing in that story.

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:06.060
Huh. I'll stay out of the loop right now.

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:10.460
That's cool. All right, so that's Marble Madness.

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:22.520
There are no other sequels or spinoffs of this game, although I guess you could say, you know, we listed Super Monkey Ball, and I'm sure there's a bunch of other games where you play either as balls or marbles or what have you.

00:52:22.520 --> 00:52:27.880
So Kirby's Dream Course 2 also feels like an inspired spiritual successor, right, Joe?

00:52:29.060 --> 00:52:40.800
Yeah, definitely. I mean, it's, you got a different goal in Kirby's Dream Course, for sure, but it's, like, the same kind of, same sort of physics and the same sort of, like, it utilizes a lot of the same elements.

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:43.560
The inspiration is definitely there, though.

00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:47.380
And, of course, Marble Madness would get ported to everything, not just the NES.

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:53.320
Yes. Weirdly enough, it did get a port to the Game Boy Advance where they removed the last three levels.

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:55.100
So there's just three levels.

00:52:55.220 --> 00:52:57.260
Oh, my God. And people paid for that.

00:52:58.100 --> 00:53:16.940
That's bizarre. Obviously, six levels is better, but I still think that there's just something about this game that maybe, you know, maybe I'm the only one who kind of saw it this way, but it was, like, even with just so few levels, I think the fun of this game is, like, mastering those levels, getting better at, you know, at controlling this marble on the physics.

00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:26.000
Regardless of, like, you know, it would always be better to have more levels, but I think I could, I think, like, if I were to get really into this, I could sink a lot of time into these six levels and even three levels.

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:27.840
What if it only included the title screen?

00:53:28.060 --> 00:53:28.920
I would play that.

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:31.420
That's all I want. It'd be better than Stack Up.

00:53:32.980 --> 00:53:35.220
It's true, because Stack Up is not a video game.

00:53:35.340 --> 00:53:37.440
Maybe by definition it is, but whatever.

00:53:37.880 --> 00:53:44.220
All right. That's Marble Madness, and that's Mark Cerny, and, yes, he does not have any gambling debts.

00:53:44.340 --> 00:53:45.720
Please don't sue me for slander.

00:53:46.860 --> 00:53:54.400
It's time for something that nobody on the internet will slander, and that is the Essential Games List.

00:53:56.960 --> 00:54:06.600
Sean, regardless of the fact that you have Marble Madness and that you also may own a copy of Marble Madness,

00:54:06.820 --> 00:54:14.320
don't let that influence your vote on the Essential Games List for Marble Madness.

00:54:15.100 --> 00:54:16.440
Well, I'm not going to lie.

00:54:16.640 --> 00:54:27.360
It's going to be hard, because this game did ruin my life, and I'll feel the effects of this sickness for at least the rest of my 30s.

00:54:28.720 --> 00:54:35.540
But I think that just the size of this game, I know that it's all about mastery.

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:42.940
And I think that that is enough for me to say that this is not an Essential game.

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:46.300
I can't just play the six levels over and over.

00:54:47.480 --> 00:54:49.700
